'''''Senna didymobotrya''''' is a species of flowering plant in the [[Fabaceae|legume family]] known by the common names '''African senna''',<ref>{{PLANTS|id=SEDI8|taxon=Senna didymobotrya|accessdate=10 November 2015}}</ref> '''popcorn senna''', '''candelabra tree''', and '''peanut butter cassia'''. It is native to Africa, where it can be found across the continent in several types of habitats.

It has been [[introduced species|introduced]] to many other parts of the world for use as an [[ornamental plant]], a [[cover crop]], and a leguminous [[green manure]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.worldagroforestrycentre.org/sea/Products/AFDbases/af/asp/SpeciesInfo.asp?SpID=18133 |title=World Agroforestry Centre |access-date=2010-07-25 |archive-date=2012-03-24 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120324073744/http://www.worldagroforestrycentre.org/sea/Products/AFDbases/af/asp/SpeciesInfo.asp?SpID=18133 }}</ref> In some places it is now naturalized in the wild, for example, in parts of [[Indonesia]], [[Australia]], [[Mexico]], and the United States in [[California]], [[Florida]], and [[Hawaii]].

==Description==
African senna is a hairy, aromatic [[shrub]] usually growing up to about five meters tall but known to reach nine meters in optimal growing conditions. The leaves of the plant are up to half a meter long and are made up of many pairs of elongated oval leaflets each up to 6.5 centimeters long.

The African senna plant has a strong, distinct scent that has been variously described as being reminiscent of [[mouse|mice]], wet [[dog]], [[peanut butter]], and [[popcorn|burnt popcorn]].<ref>Schmelzer, G. H. and A. Gurib-Fakim. (2008). [https://books.google.com/books?id=7FJqgQ3_tnUC&q=Senna+didymobotrya Plant Resources of Tropical Africa 1: Medicinal Plants.] page 507.</ref>

The plant flowers plentifully in [[raceme]]s of bright yellow flowers, with some flowers also occurring in leaf axils. The flower raceme has open flowers on the lower part with unopened buds at the tip covered in stark brownish green or black [[bract]]s. The flower has five concave petals each 1.5 to nearly 3 centimeters long. The flower has ten [[stamen]]s, usually seven fertile ones and three sterile [[staminode]]s. Some of the stamens have large anthers measuring a centimeter long.

The fruits of the African senna plant are flat brown [[legume]] pods up to 12 centimeters long, each containing as many as 16 bean-like seeds that are themselves up to a centimeter long each.

The plant is poisonous.<ref>GRIN-link and Blundell, M. 1987. Wild Flowers of East Africa. page 92.</ref>

==Taxonomy and phylogenetics==
[[File:Senna didymobotrya Coonoor ph 00.jpg|thumb|A small tree in [[Tamil Nadu]], India]]
